---
/**
 * The compliance page: the statutory engine and the watch on legislative change.
 *
 * This page names a real limit, which is unusual for a marketing page and is
 * the point. Several hundred fields are read and several hundred are not, and
 * the engine stops rather than acting on law nobody has read. Saying so is what
 * makes the rest of it credible.
 *
 * The coverage table is written by hand for now. It should be generated from
 * ParcelHOA's src/lib/statute/data so it cannot go stale in prose.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import Band from "../components/Band.astro";

const FIELDS = [
  "A records response window",
  "A meeting notice floor",
  "An election notice period",
  "An architectural response period",
  "A reserve study cycle",
  "A reserve funding requirement",
  "A collection procedure",
  "A payment allocation order",
  "A list of records exempt from production",
];
---

<Base
  title="Compliance"
  description="Parcel models HOA statute across every state, tracks the bills that would change it, and tells you what a change means for your association. Where a provision has not been read, it stops rather than guessing."
  shareTitle="It reads the law, then watches it change"
  path="/compliance"
  reviewed="August 2026"
>
  <Hero eyebrow="Compliance">
    <h1 class="t-display">It reads the law, then watches it change</h1>
    <p class="t-lede">
      An association is governed by two things: its own documents, and the statute of the state it
      sits in. The documents do not move often. The statute moves every session.
    </p>
  </Hero>

  <Band title="What is modelled">
    <p>
      Statute across all fifty states, plus the District of Columbia, Puerto Rico and the US
      Virgin Islands.
    </p>
    <p>
      Not one record per state. One record per rule. A jurisdiction has:
    </p>
    <ul class="rows">{FIELDS.map((f) => <li>{f}</li>)}</ul>
    <p class="coda">
      Each is held separately, because they change separately. Every value carries the section it
      came from and the date it was read.
    </p>
  </Band>

  <Band title="Three answers, not two" field>
    <ul class="rows">
      <li><b>Read, and there is a rule.</b> The value, with its citation.</li>
      <li><b>Read, and there is no rule.</b> Also useful, and it carries the citation of the section that is silent, because a legislature imposing nothing is a claim about the law and you should be able to check it.</li>
      <li><b>Not read yet.</b> Carries nothing, because there is nothing to say.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="coda">
      That third state is the one that matters. A field saying no requirement lets a feature run.
      A field saying nobody has looked has to switch it off. Held the obvious way, as an empty
      box, those two are identical, and every piece of code downstream treats them the same. That
      is how software ends up asserting a legal position nobody took.
    </p>
  </Band>

  <Band title="What happens when the law changes">
    <p>
      Parcel watches the bills that would move a rule it holds. When one passes, you get told what
      changed, which of your deadlines it touches, and what Parcel has already adjusted.
    </p>
    <p class="coda">
      A notice period moving from fourteen days to twenty-one is not a memo you read and file. It
      is a date on your calendar that has to move, and every notice counted back from it.
    </p>
  </Band>

  <Band title="Where it refuses" field>
    <div class="panel--statutory">
      <p class="t-label">It fails closed</p>
      <p class="lede">
        An unread provision does not fall back to a default. The feature that needs it does not
        run, and the interface names the provision it is missing.
      </p>
      <p class="note">
        This is a real limit and worth being plain about. Several hundred fields have been read
        and several hundred have not. The product tells you which for your state, when you sign
        up rather than in a notice.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="coda">
      The alternative is worse. Software that gives a Colorado association Florida's notice
      periods produces a letter that looks correct, cites a statute that does not apply, and goes
      out on a period that is wrong.
    </p>
  </Band>

  <Band title="Coverage" note="See documents and answers" noteHref="/product/documents-and-answers">
    <p>
      Every jurisdiction, what has been read, and what has not. Generated from the same data the
      product runs on, so it moves when the reading moves.
    </p>
    <div class="panel--enforcement">
      <p class="t-label">Coming with the next release</p>
      <p>
        The per-jurisdiction table is generated from the statutory data rather than written here,
        so it cannot fall out of step. Until that generator ships this page states the shape and
        not the counts.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Band>

  <Band title="What this is not" field>
    <p>
      Parcel is not a law firm and does not give legal advice. It records what the statute says,
      where it says it, and when it was read. Your attorney reads it for your association.
    </p>
  </Band>

  <section class="close on-ink">
    <div class="page">
      <h2 class="t-sheet">Ask what has been read for your state.</h2>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn btn--on-ink" href="/demo">Get early access</a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost-on-ink" href="/contact">Email the founder</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .close { background: var(--cold); color: var(--paper); border-top: 1.5px solid var(--terracotta); }
  .close h2 { color: var(--paper); max-width: 26ch; }
  .close .actions { margin-top: var(--s-6); }
  h1 { max-width: 18ch; }
</style>
